Creating a web server in C involves handling sockets, HTTP requests, and responses. Below is a simple example of a web server that can handle GET requests and serve HTML files. This example uses the `libmicrohttpd` library, which simplifies HTTP server implementation.

First, install the `libmicrohttpd` library if you haven't already:

```sh
sudo apt-get install libmicrohttpd-dev
```

Then, you can use the following code to create a basic web server:

```c
#include <microhttpd.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

#define PORT 8080

static int
answer_to_connection(void *cls, struct MHD_Connection *connection,
                     const char *url, const char *method,
                     const char *version, const char *upload_data,
                     size_t *upload_data_size, void **con_cls)
{
    struct MHD_Response *response;
    int ret;
    const char *html = "<html><body><h1>Hello, World!</h1></body></html>";

    response = MHD_create_response_from_buffer(strlen(html), (void *)html, MHD_RESPMEM_PERSISTENT);
    MHD_add_response_header(response, "Content-Type", "text/html");
    ret = MHD_queue_response(connection, MHD_HTTP_OK, response);
    MHD_destroy_response(response);

    return ret;
}

int
main(void)
{
    struct MHD_Daemon *daemon;

    daemon = MHD_start_daemon(MHD_USE_SELECT_INTERNALLY, PORT, NULL, NULL,
                             &answer_to_connection, NULL, MHD_OPTION_END);
    if (daemon == NULL)
    {
        return 1;
    }

    MHD_run_daemon(daemon);
    MHD_stop_daemon(daemon);

    return 0;
}
```

To compile the code, use the following command:

```sh
gcc -o simple_web_server simple_web_server.c -lmicrohttpd
```

Run the server with:

```sh
./simple_web_server
```

This server will listen on port 8080 and respond with a simple HTML page. You can expand this example to handle more complex requests and serve different types of content.
